// Slate -- a small indentation-structured language, written in sysl to find out what sysl is like
// to write a real front end in.
//
// The language is a means rather than an end. What is being tested is sysl: a tree-walking
// interpreter is one of the few programs that reaches for nearly everything a language has --
// recursive data through references, payload-carrying enums, traits with associated types,
// generics over a container, closures that outlive the frame that made them, and an error path
// that has to carry a position from the byte that caused it all the way to the sentence a person
// reads. Where sysl makes one of those awkward, that is the finding, and the finding is the point.
//
// The module a program imports is `slate`, and this repository is not an org package: nothing
// depends on it by coordinate, so it claims the short name rather than a reverse-DNS one.

package {
  name    = "slate"
  version = "0.0.1"

  // The oldest compiler this builds with. `sh.sysl.parsing` states 0.0.76 as its own floor and a
  // consumer of it cannot ask for less; nothing here needs anything newer than that yet.
  sysl = "0.0.76"
}

// **The reader is `sh.sysl.parsing`'s, and reaching for `layout` is deliberate.** That module -- the
// column stack that turns indentation into brackets -- had no consumer in the org when this project
// started: `json` has no line structure and ogol has no indentation at all. A language whose blocks
// are indented runs is the consumer it was written for, so this exercises the one tier of the
// toolkit nothing had yet run.
dependencies {
  parsing { git = "github.com/sysl-lang/parsing", version = "0.2.1" }
}

// **`heap = true`.** An interpreter grows a token list, a syntax tree and an environment chain while
// it reads, and none of the three has a size known before the input does. Slate is a host program
// and will not be linked into a board, so nothing is being given up here.
requires {
  heap = true
}
